+\textit{Scope.} Every conclusion here is bounded to what was tested. The generator is LAKE-RED, run once, offline,
+before any training; it never sees the score. Its pool re-uses each of 4,447 HKU-IS foregrounds with a new background,
+so the allocation chooses among background variations of foregrounds the base pool already contains. Two renders of
+the same foreground on different backgrounds share most of their embedding variance (intraclass correlation $0.71$ in
+frozen DINOv2 features). In 52 of the 75 target clusters, the nearest available foreground cut-out lies farther from
+the cluster's centre than the cluster's median real image, partly because a cut-out on a plain background and a full
+photograph differ as images. The allocation acts on $k = 75$ $k$-means clusters of frozen self-supervised features,
+with a budget of $N = 1000$ images, for SINet and SINet-v2 trained with two-round self-training at a pinned number of
+steps, and is read on COD10K-test and NC4K. We tested allocation, not generation: a generator that synthesises new
+foregrounds for the hardest clusters, or one steered by the score while it generates, is outside this experiment, and
+we make no claim about other generators.
